+# Export acceptance and restart recovery hunt
+
+Checked revision: `6a3927e69761ad3e4a04be827806cbbed380ff4b`.
+Contract: [app/export-contract.md](app/export-contract.md).
+Witness: [tests/hunt_witness.py](tests/hunt_witness.py).
+
+Scope: `Queue.submit`, `Queue.work`, startup recovery, and durable jobs/results.
+Prioritized harm: accepted work permanently abandoned after interruption or a
+failed output transaction. Stopping boundary: this operation and its direct
+recovery path; no broader note-store or import audit.
+
+## Demonstrated defect: committed running jobs are never recovered
+
+An accepted job can permanently lose its promised result after process exit.
+`submit` commits the pending row before returning acceptance
+(`app/export_queue.py:21–25`). `work` commits `running` before producing output
+(`:33–38`). Termination at the documented `after_claim` boundary therefore leaves
+a durable running job with no result. On restart, the constructor calls
+`recover`, which does nothing (`:15–19`). Every subsequent `work` selects only
+pending rows (`:28–32`). The accepted job remains inaccessible to the worker.
+
+The witness submits `job` / `payload` in a child process, calls real `work`, and
+terminates with `os._exit(23)` in the documented hook. Only after that process
+has exited does the parent reopen the same SQLite file and call `work` three
+times. Observed: no result, durable state `running`; expected: `payload` after
+healthy recovery. The SQL selection predicate and absent recovery establish
+that further healthy calls cannot progress this row; the finding does not
+infer permanence merely from a finite retry count.
+
+The same mechanism affects an output transaction error: the saved witness uses
+a temporary SQLite trigger to abort the result insert, removes the trigger,
+then retries real `work`. The result is still absent. This demonstrates the
+post-claim SQL-error path, not an actual operating-system I/O fault injection.
+The code ordering also leaves running state committed when the output
+transaction rolls back. Both schedules exercise one defect, not two distinct
+findings.
+
+Fix direction: reconstruct unfinished running obligations at startup and ensure
+failed attempts become eligible for retry in the live process. Under the
+supported single-worker, sequential-restart contract, unfinished running rows
+can be returned to pending. Preserve the atomic result-insert/done transaction
+and avoid replaying completed jobs. No repair was made.
+
+## Coverage and execution
+
+Executed `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 tests/hunt_witness.py` with Python
+3.9.6. Exit status **1**, four tests run: two passed and two failed at contract
+assertions. Each test uses an isolated temporary local SQLite file. The child
+exit status is checked so a failed setup cannot masquerade as a crash witness.
+
+| Selected case | Observed result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Acceptance committed, process exits before work | Restart produces the original payload; passed |
+| Running committed, process exits before output | Restart abandons accepted job; failed |
+| Output transaction aborts, fault removed, healthy retries | Accepted job remains abandoned; failed |
+| Completed transaction, process exits, same-payload resubmission | Result survives; no further work or duplicate result; passed |
+
+Code inspection: acceptance uses a committed SQLite transaction; result creation
+and the done transition share one transaction. No other queue consumers or
+recovery owners were found by targeted searches for `Queue`, `export_queue`,
+`submit`, and `recover` in application code and governing records.
+
+Governing-record discovery covered root `specs/` and references into the named
+surface. `GATE-local-only` applies and was respected. `ARCH-app`,
+`SPEC-note-payload`, and `REQ-import-source` describe adjacent note/import paths,
+not queue recovery. `CLAIM-single-writer` concerns writes under `data/`; this
+queue witness uses temporary SQLite files outside that directory. No claim
+proof or historical verification evidence was needed or changed. The supported
+export contract directly supplies the obligation tested here.
+
+Assumptions are the contract's local durable SQLite storage, exactly one active
+worker, no overlapping requests, restart after the prior worker stops, fixed
+payload per job ID, and eventual healthy repeated work calls. Storage loss,
+corruption, concurrency, and conflicting payloads are outside this contract.
+No power-loss simulation, mid-SQLite-commit kill, or actual transient OS I/O
+failure was tested. SQLite commit durability/atomicity is trusted as specified
+by the supplied contract. This is a deterministic implementation witness, not
+a generated search or an independent claim verification. No claim of exhaustive
+bug absence is made. Product code, records, and existing evidence were preserved.
